positioning:
Muse showed that people want an agent that does real work with strong guardrails. It is also a separate app, available only in the US, requires a card, works 1:1 only, and users call it data-hungry. Mira showed how far Telegram distribution goes: 1.17M MAU, with a third of new users arriving from groups. But it runs about 95% of its tokens on cheap open-weight models, has no documented approvals, audit log or memory viewer, makes group facts visible to members, and only 1 in 250 of its users ever connects a tool. folk has the richest feature set, but it sends Telegram users to a web dashboard, asks for approval via typed /approve, writes lowercase emails, and puts its best features behind a paywall.

Gora wins by not being a bot you go and visit. It is built from Telegram's own 2026 primitives, which none of the three uses fully:
- live rich drafts with Telegram's Stop button;
- a private topic for each task;
- Chat Automation that triages and drafts replies inside your own DMs, with consent per chat;
- @gora guest replies in any chat that are built so private data never enters them;
- an inline-mode toolbox;
- ephemeral answers in groups that only the asker sees;
- reactions as silent acknowledgements;
- native checklists and live location;
- a Mini App control center with a full ledger and memory you can forget item by item;
- Stars subscriptions, with no card, worldwide.

Underneath is Muse-grade trust implemented in code rather than in the prompt:
- a Sentinel policy layer;
- approvals that only the owner's signed button tap can grant;
- an append-only audit ledger;
- encryption at rest;
- consent handling that follows the Telegram Business ToS.

The model is Claude Opus 5. In one line: Muse's trust, Mira's reach and folk's proactivity, living inside Telegram instead of next to it.
